  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 PM CDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nCurrent Conditions:\n\nAnother quiet day across the region with surface high pressure \nslowly departing through the day. Satellite already shows increasing \ncloud cover spilling over from the west as the upper level ridge \nbegins to flatten. The late arrival of the clouds plus the return of \nsoutherly winds will see temperatures a couple degrees higher today \nthan yesterday with highs in the 70s and a few spots climbing into \nthe low 80s. \n\nWeekend:\n\nFriday night cross barrier flow over the Rockies leads to the \ndevelopment of a surface low pressure over the Northern Plains. This \nsystem is projected to slide across SD and into southern MN with a \nstrong low level jet ramping up through the early morning hours. The \nNorthland should find itself on the tip of the nose of the jet which \nwill promote some scattered showers and storms across our southern \ncounties. The better forcing and dynamics for more robust activity \nstays to our south with the REFS keeping our precipitation totals \nfairly minimal and generally less than 0.10\" If a storm does manage \nto develop expect some locally higher amounts. The orientation of \nthe low level jet will ween itself out of the area through the day \nSaturday with the overall strength decreasing as well. We will still \nmaintain some low end PoPs of 20-30% to account for the isentropic\nlift across the area but overall expect coverage and activity \nto decrease as it shifts to the west through the afternoon. \n\nModels still remain in flux for Sunday's synoptic set up with the \nlatest showing quite the decrease in PoPs. The warm front that was \noriginally forecasted to be draped across northern MN has drifted \nmuch farther north leaving the Northland in the gap between systems. \nThe GFS IVT also highlights the southern Pacific moisture tracking \nto our south and being wrapped up with the southern low moving \nacross the Midwest. Overall, the PoPs have been decreased down to 20-\n30% and based on the latest CAMs and soundings it wouldn't be too \nsurprising if that downward trend continues in subsequent\nforecast packages.  \n\nEarly next week:\n\nAn upper level low matures over the Canadian Prairies late Sunday into \nearly next week and will likely bring out next round of \nprecipitation to the Northland. With the deepening low to the north \nand a secondary low moving across central MN we will see an occluded \nfront draped over the region and pushing east through Monday. A \nstrengthening upper level jet will increase deep layer shear. The \narea will also sport some MUCAPE values in excess of 500 J/kg. \nPairing these two we could see some strong storms develop. Will wait \nfor more consistent model runs before diving into more details of \nthreats and timing. \n\nTuesday's forecast is highly dependent on the progression of the \nfrontal boundary on Monday. The GFS would suggest that it stalls \nout over NW WI while the Euro sags it further south out of our \nCWA. Both models do highlight a shortwave trough that will be \nentering from the west that day, so depending on where the front\nlies there will be additional rain and storm chances along the\nboundary.\n\nWednesday looks like a brief pause in the rain chances as high \npressure moves over the region. This is fairly short lived as \nensembles highlight another shortwave moving across the Northern \nPlains for Thursday.",
